# Coach speech (Brazilian Portuguese): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Brazilian Portuguese needs.
# Register: the coach says "você" to the player, as Brazilians do (never "tu" or "o senhor"), and "seu /
# sua" for the player's things; the coach is male ("estou contente", "obrigado"). The player's gender is
# unknown: no adjective or participle agreeing with the player ("pronto", "bem-vindo", "derrotado"):
# rephrase with a noun or an impersonal turn ("Tudo pronto?", "Boas-vindas!", "Você perdeu").
# Written text keeps the standard forms of written Brazilian ("para", "está"; not "pra", "tá", "né").
# Vocabulary as assets/i18n/pt-BR.lang: lance, casa, coluna, fileira, xeque, xeque-mate, roque,
# afogamento, empate, cravada, garfo, espeto, erro grave, erro, imprecisão.
# The voice is the synthesizer's Portuguese voice: it reads this Brazilian text as written.

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns and genders: o peão, o cavalo, o bispo, a torre, a dama, o rei ("dama" as in Brazilian chess
# books and notation, never "rainha"). Portuguese nouns do not decline: the forms differ by the
# article, its contraction with a preposition (de + o = do, em + o = no, a + o = ao, por + o = pelo),
# the possessive, and the pronoun that stands for the piece. Each form is named after its masculine
# word; the program says the feminine word for a rook or a queen.
# The player's pieces: "seu cavalo / sua torre"; the coach's: "meu cavalo / minha torre"; the Analysis
# mode names a piece by its side, with the article: "o cavalo branco", "a torre preta".
# Forms (every one defined for all six pieces and the four owners your, my, white, black):
#   nom       seu cavalo / sua torre, meu bispo / minha dama; white, black: o rei branco / a torre preta.
#             Subject or direct object; Brazilians leave out the article before seu / meu:
#             "{your} não anda assim." "Eu tomo {your}." "{piece} fica sem defesa."
#   art       o seu cavalo / a sua torre, o meu ... (white, black: as nom). After a preposition that
#             does not contract (com, para, sem, contra, entre, sobre, até) or where the article reads
#             better: "Jogue com {your:art}." "{your:esse} é {your:art}."
#   do        do seu cavalo / da sua torre, do cavalo branco (de + article): "Pense na segurança {your:do}."
#   no        no seu cavalo / na sua torre, no cavalo branco (em + article): "Fique de olho {your:no}."
#   ao        ao seu cavalo / à sua torre, ao cavalo branco (a + article): "Dê atenção {your:ao}."
#   pelo      pelo seu cavalo / pela sua torre, pelo cavalo branco (por + article):
#             "{your} é atacad{your:o} {my:pelo}."
#   Never write de, em, a or por right before a piece placeholder: use do / no / ao / pelo ("de {piece}"
#   would give "de o cavalo branco", and "de seu cavalo" is stiff).
#   the       o cavalo / a torre: no possessive and no side, where the owner is obvious:
#             "Você moveu {your:the} duas vezes."
#   the_do    do cavalo / da torre: "A diagonal {my:the_do} está aberta."
#   the_no    no cavalo / na torre: "Pense primeiro {your:the_no}."
#   the_ao    ao cavalo / à torre: "Dê espaço {your:the_ao}."
#   the_pelo  pelo cavalo / pela torre: "Comece {your:the_pelo}."
#   noun      cavalo / torre, the bare noun: "Outro lance de {your:noun}?" (= "Outro lance de bispo?")
#   ele       ele / ela, the piece as subject, or after com, para, sem, sobre, contra, entre:
#             "{your:esse} é {your:art}. {your:ele} anda na diagonal."
#   dele      dele / dela, "of it", "its" (de + ele): "{t1} está atrás {your:dele}."
#   nele      nele / nela, "on it", "in it" (em + ele): "Fique de olho {my:nele}."
#   o         o / a, three uses: the object pronoun after a verb ending in a vowel ("Capture-{my:o}!")
#             or before the verb ("Eu {your:o} tomo."); and the ending of an adjective or participle
#             that agrees with the piece, written after the piece: "{your} está cravad{your:o}." =
#             "cravado" / "cravada" (the same way pres-, sozinh-, protegid-, atacad-, perdid-, expost-,
#             encurralad- + {your:o}; adjectives in -e do not change: livre, forte).
#   lo        lo / la, the object pronoun after an infinitive that drops its r: "Você pode capturá-{my:lo}."
#             (= "capturá-lo" / "capturá-la"; also tomá-, salvá-, defendê-, protegê-). After a verb
#             ending in a nasal sound ("tomam-no") rephrase: "o / a" before the verb.
#   este      este / esta, "this one", for the coach's own pieces (near him): "{my:este} é {my:art}."
#   esse      esse / essa, "that one / this one" near the listener, for the player's pieces:
#             "{your:esse} é {your:art}."
#   "lhe" and "se" do not change with the piece: they need no form.
# Placeholders at the start of a sentence are capitalised by the program ("Seu cavalo", "Ela", "Esse").
# Two pieces together ("{t1} e {t2}") have no plural form (their genders may differ): make the words
# agree with "peças" ("as duas peças", "uma das peças") or use a phrase that does not agree ("estão
# sob ataque", "{t1} e {t2} caem").
